1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:02,387
Why We Tell Stories.

2
00:00:02,337 --> 00:00:05,425
Storytelling predates written language.

3
00:00:05,425 --> 00:00:12,175
Oral narratives preserved history, taught moral lessons, and explained natural phenomena long before writing.

4
00:00:12,175 --> 00:00:19,075
The oldest known written narrative, the Epic of Gilgamesh, dates from Mesopotamia over 4,000 years ago.

5
00:00:19,075 --> 00:00:29,000
Most narratives follow a pattern Joseph Campbell termed the hero's journey: a protagonist departs, faces trials, acquires wisdom, and returns transformed.

6
00:00:29,000 --> 00:00:34,262
This structure recurs across myths, novels, and films from every continent.

7
00:00:34,262 --> 00:00:41,650
Humans organize memories as narratives; news, entertainment, and self-understanding all take story form.

8
00:00:41,650 --> 00:00:49,225
This cognitive framework appears universal across cultures, suggesting that narrative thinking is fundamental to human cognition.

9
00:00:49,225 --> 00:00:57,637
Oral traditions, from Homeric epics to African griot histories, preserved collective knowledge across generations before writing existed.

